        <ArticleTitle>Robotic laparoscopic surgery with the da Vinci® system: an early experience.</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>70-4</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText>Theoretically, robotic surgery is likely to overcome the intrinsic limitations of laparoscopic surgery, such as a view not under the direct surgeon's control, two-dimensional imaging, and limited motion of instruments. In this chapter, the preliminary experience with a robotic laparoscopic surgery system in the Surgical Department of the Alessandria Hospital, Italy, is reported. From November 2005 to August 2007, a total of 162 laparoscopic robotic operations were performed, in which robotic surgery was found to be feasible and safe. It takes a longer time than standard laparoscopy but seems able to reduce the need of a steep learning curve.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
